+### Final softmax statistics
+
+Plan with `lse_mode="base2"` or `"basee"` and run with `return_lse=True`
+to receive `(output, lse)`. LSE is compact FP32 with the query shape minus
+the last dimension. An optional caller `lse` buffer is written even when
+`return_lse=False`; supplying both outputs supports allocation-free graph
+replay with validation disabled. The default `lse_mode="none"` keeps the
+output-only path. Final stores remove internal FP8 probability amplification;
+intermediate split statistics retain their native reduction convention.
+LSE excludes output scaling. Buffers must not overlap live inputs, each other,
+or workspace.
+
+Scalar FP8 dequantization is caller-composed: for stored Q/K/V with scales
+`s_q`, `s_k`, `s_v`, use effective QK scale `sm_scale * s_q * s_k` and output
+scale `s_v * desired_output_scale`. Context planning calls these `sm_scale`
+and `output_scale`; context run accepts a one-element FP32
+`scale_softmax_log2 = effective_QK_scale * log2(e)` and `output_scale`.
+FMHA/MLA decode call them `bmm1_scale` and `bmm2_scale`. Per-tensor scalar
+scales are not automatically inferred from FP8 storage. Packed NVFP4 block
+scales remain a separate input contract.
+
+Plan with `use_attention_sinks=True` and bind `attention_sinks` on each run.
+The CUDA FP32 vector `[Hq]` holds sink logits in natural-log score units after
+QK scaling. Each sink adds denominator mass and zero V contribution. Finite
+values and `-inf` are accepted; NaN and positive infinity are rejected when
+validation is enabled. Final normalization merges sink mass stably before
+output rounding, exactly once after any split reduction. LSE includes the
+sink mass. Default plans compile away this feature; values remain runtime
+inputs and may change between completed graph replays.
+
+
+### Runtime split-KV policy
+
+Split counts and maximum counts are private policy state. The hardware ceiling
+defaults to the device's physical SM count (152 on the validation GB300).
+The policy bounds useful work using planned geometry and KV capacity, then
+prepares scratch/reduction capacity in ranges 1, 2–4, 5–8, 9–16, 17–32, 33–64,
+sixteen-split ranges above64 clipped to the hardware ceiling. A range boundary is not an
+architectural limit. No user count/ceiling or count tensor is required.
+
+Requested counts are typed runtime launch operands in producers and reducers.
+Scratch strides and cluster/barrier participation retain prepared capacity;
+inactive slots/ranks contribute neutral state and preserve teardown. Count
+changes within a prepared capacity reuse the compiled callables, including
+reduction. Static internal kernel recipes remain available for comparison.
+
+MLA 2CTA runtime plans size scratch from the prepared policy bound, replacing
+the former implicit128-split reservation. Parallel reduction eligibility is
+retained for runtime counts and selected from the capacity/topology envelope.
+The host launcher prepares exact integer-division metadata once per call and
+shares it with scheduled tile consumers. Full Q128 runtime-split graphs reserve
+eight additional registers per Softmax thread from the correction warpgroup.
+
+Dense FMHA prepares two page-ID schedules together. If the host KV bound and
+runtime count guarantee that the complete instruction-aligned CTA span fits in
+32 page IDs, one CTA-relative window is retained through K and V. Longer spans
+use the staged pipeline. The selection stays inside the compiled launcher, so
+switching counts or crossing this span bound requires no additional JIT. The
+window origin follows the CTA, including splits that start inside an aligned
+32-ID region. Scratch-buffer sizing and ownership do not depend on this choice.
